<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-Belgian India Rubber Company</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1892">1892</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as ABIR</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anglo-Belgian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">Rubber</a> Company, known as ABIR, was a company founded in 1892 that received a concession for rubber in the Lopori and Maringa basins of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a>, where the state held a large share. It enforced rubber quotas through armed sentries, hostage-taking and killings, becoming one of the most notorious agents of the Congo atrocities until the state took over its concession in 1906.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-belgian-india-rubber-company.jpg" alt="Anglo-Belgian India Rubber Company" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edward Linley Sambourne · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Punch_congo_rubber_cartoon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
